import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj07.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Japan's Corporate Split System (会社分割) — the Backdrop" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Core Question — Has the Divesting Entity's Investment Genuinely Changed?" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Consideration Received — Cash/Assets vs Shares in the Transferee" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Continuing Involvement — Partial or No Gain Recognition" },
        { id: "s7", label: "A Clean Break — Full Gain/Loss Recognition" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — a Clean Divestiture to an Unrelated Third Party" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Receiving Shares in the Transferee, Retaining Significant Influence" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The divesting entity's own accounting, for Japan's distinctive corporate restructuring tools</div>
        <div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 7 addresses accounting from the perspective of an entity DIVESTING (transferring) a business to another entity — via a CORPORATE SPLIT (会社分割), sale of business, or share-for-share exchange. It is the natural COMPANION to ASBJ Statement No. 21 (Business Combinations), which addresses the ACQUIRING entity's side of the SAME transaction — together, the two Statements provide a complete J-GAAP accounting framework for Japan's genuinely DISTINCTIVE corporate reorganisation techniques.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Entities transferring a business to another entity</div>
        <div class="principle">This Statement applies to entities TRANSFERRING a business (or a portion of a business) to ANOTHER entity, whether through a corporate split, a sale of business assets/liabilities, or a share exchange arrangement.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Japan's Corporate Split System (会社分割) — the Backdrop</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A distinctively Japanese legal restructuring mechanism</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Japan's 2005 COMPANIES ACT (会社法) reform SIGNIFICANTLY expanded the "CORPORATE SPLIT" (会社分割) system — a LEGAL mechanism allowing a company to TRANSFER all or part of a business (including its associated assets, liabilities, contracts, and employees) to ANOTHER company (existing or newly created), often WITHOUT requiring individual asset-by-asset transfer procedures. This tool became WIDELY used for Japanese corporate group reorganisations, spin-offs, and joint venture formations — creating the NEED for dedicated accounting guidance addressing BOTH sides of such transactions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Core Question — Has the Divesting Entity's Investment Genuinely Changed?</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Distinguishing a genuine sale from a continuing investment</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">The CENTRAL analytical question is whether the divesting entity has GENUINELY ended its economic involvement with the divested business, or whether — through the CONSIDERATION received (e.g. shares in the transferee) — it EFFECTIVELY retains an ONGOING investment in that same underlying business. This distinction DRIVES whether a full GAIN or LOSS is recognised, or whether recognition is LIMITED (or entirely withheld).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Consideration Received — Cash/Assets vs Shares in the Transferee</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The form of consideration is the key analytical driver</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where the divesting entity receives CASH or OTHER ASSETS UNRELATED to the transferee's own equity, the transaction is generally treated as a genuine SALE — the divested business's carrying amounts are DERECOGNISED, and a GAIN or LOSS is recognised for the difference between consideration received and carrying amount. Where the divesting entity instead receives SHARES IN THE TRANSFEREE, the analysis becomes MORE NUANCED (Section 6), since the entity may still hold an ONGOING economic interest in the SAME underlying business, just through a DIFFERENT ownership vehicle.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Continuing Involvement — Partial or No Gain Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising a gain only to the extent of the genuinely divested portion</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where the divesting entity RECEIVES shares in the TRANSFEREE and, as a result, retains SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE or JOINT CONTROL over the transferee (or the transferee remains, in substance, an affiliated entity), the transaction is viewed as ONLY PARTIALLY a genuine sale — a gain is recognised ONLY to the extent the underlying business has effectively been sold to GENUINELY OUTSIDE parties, with the REMAINING portion treated as a CONTINUING investment carried forward at its PRE-TRANSACTION carrying amount, rather than restated to fair value. If the divesting entity retains CONTROL or the transaction otherwise doesn't represent a genuine change in economic substance, NO gain may be recognised at all.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>A Clean Break — Full Gain/Loss Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Genuine sales to unrelated third parties</div>
        <div class="text-supporting">Where the divesting entity has NO continuing involvement or influence over the divested business AFTER the transaction (a genuine SALE to an UNRELATED third party, receiving cash or non-equity consideration), the FULL gain or loss is recognised — the divested business's carrying amounts are DERECOGNISED entirely, with the difference against consideration received flowing THROUGH the Statement of Income.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — a Clean Divestiture to an Unrelated Third Party</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Full gain recognition on a genuine sale</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Selling a business division for cash</div><div class="wk-body">A company sells a business division (carrying amount of net assets: ¥1,200,000,000) to an entirely unrelated buyer for ¥1,600,000,000 cash, with no continuing involvement in the divested business thereafter.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company recognises a <strong>¥400,000,000 GAIN</strong> (¥1,600,000,000 − ¥1,200,000,000) IMMEDIATELY in the Statement of Income — a genuine, clean divestiture with NO continuing economic interest retained.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Receiving Shares in the Transferee, Retaining Significant Influence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Only a partial gain, reflecting continuing involvement</div>
        <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A corporate split into a joint venture</div><div class="wk-body">A company transfers a business division (carrying amount ¥800,000,000) via a corporate split into a NEW joint venture entity, receiving a 40% equity stake (SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE, but not control) in the joint venture in return, with the joint venture's OTHER 60% held by a genuinely unrelated partner.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the company RETAINS significant influence over the SAME underlying business (now held through its 40% joint venture stake), a gain is recognised ONLY to the extent of the 60% GENUINELY divested to the unrelated partner — with the REMAINING 40% "interest" carried forward at its PROPORTIONATE historical carrying amount, rather than restated to fair value, reflecting the CONTINUING nature of that portion of the investment.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 7 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature and terms of the business divestiture, including the consideration received</span><span>Gain or loss recognised, and the basis for any continuing involvement treatment</span><span>Nature of any continuing relationship with the transferee</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
        <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether the divesting entity retains control, significant influence, or joint control over the transferee</span><span>What portion of the transaction represents a genuine sale to outside parties versus a continuing investment</span></div></div>
        <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of consideration received, where not directly observable</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP vs IFRS</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span>J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 7)</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>General principle — control-based derecognition</span><span>Assesses whether control/significant influence is retained over the SAME underlying business</span><span>IFRS 10's control-based derecognition test, broadly similar underlying logic</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Corporate split (会社分割) as a distinct legal mechanism</span><span>A genuinely JAPAN-SPECIFIC legal restructuring tool this Statement was specifically developed to address</span><span>NO direct equivalent legal construct — IFRS addresses the ECONOMIC substance generally, without a standard built around this SPECIFIC Japanese legal mechanism</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="note">The UNDERLYING economic principle — continuing involvement limits gain recognition — is broadly CONVERGED with IFRS's general approach to control and derecognition. The SPECIFIC legal TRIGGER (Japan's corporate split system) is a genuinely DISTINCTIVE feature of the Japanese corporate law landscape this Statement was built to address.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every business divestiture event lands</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Clean divestiture (unrelated third party)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Full gain/loss recognised, business derecognised</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Divestiture with continuing involvement</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Partial gain recognised; retained portion carried at historical cost</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 21</span><span>Business Combinations — the companion standard governing the ACQUIRING entity's side of the same transactions</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 16</span><span>Equity Method of Accounting for Investments — relevant where continuing significant influence is retained</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Corporate split (会社分割)</div><div class="glossary-def">A Japanese Companies Act legal mechanism allowing a company to transfer all or part of a business to another company, often without individual asset-by-asset transfer procedures.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Continuing involvement</div><div class="glossary-def">A divesting entity's retained control, significant influence, or joint control over a divested business, limiting or eliminating gain recognition on the transaction.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表) <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書) <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部 <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書) <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div></div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
